<Project>

  <!--
    Imported into every project that (transitively) references any DatadogNet.*.Mac package -
    DatadogNet.Core.Mac is the one dependency all of them share, so this is the one place a fix
    that has to reach every consumer belongs.

    Works around dotnet/macios#21636 ("The managed static registrar only works when
    TrimMode=full"). .NET 9+ defaults Mac Catalyst and iOS-device builds to the managed-static
    registrar, which relies on a lookup type - ObjCRuntime.__Registrar__ - woven into every
    binding assembly that needs it. That weaving step only runs reliably under TrimMode=full;
    MAUI apps default to TrimMode=partial, and under partial trimming the weaving silently skips
    some assemblies. Confirmed here for DatadogNet.Logs.Mac specifically (Core.Mac got its
    __Registrar__ type, Logs.Mac did not - same package version, same build), which crashes at
    first use with:

        ObjCRuntime.RuntimeException: Could not find the type 'ObjCRuntime.__Registrar__'
        in the assembly 'DatadogNet.Logs.Mac'.

    Only on a real device or under Mac Catalyst - the managed-static registrar isn't the default
    for the simulator, which is exactly why neither this repository's own CI nor DatadogNet's
    catches it: both only ever build and run the simulator target.

    The classic static registrar doesn't need this per-assembly weaving at all, sidestepping the
    trimming interaction entirely. Verified: DatadogNetExample builds and runs on a real Mac with
    this set, where it crashed without it.

    Condition guards against overriding a consumer that has already picked a Registrar - an app
    that explicitly wants managed-static (and sets TrimMode=full itself, where this bug doesn't
    reproduce) is left alone.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition=" '$(Registrar)' == '' And $([MSBuild]::GetTargetPlatformIdentifier('$(TargetFramework)')) == 'maccatalyst' ">
    <Registrar>static</Registrar>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
